diff --git a/tests/mshow_identity/README.md b/tests/mshow_identity/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e1d877f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/mshow_identity/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# Verified modules callback regressions
+
+Run `python3 tests/mshow_identity/run.py`, optionally with `--sanitizers`.
+The harness compiles the production name matcher, modules callback, show-body
+resolver, probe-address validator, registration and teardown helpers. The
+kprobe model preserves the resolved address after registration failures and
+the disabled flag after unregister, and delivers callbacks during registration
+and teardown to verify that admission is closed at both boundaries.
+
+Twenty-three cases cover correct/duplicate targets, unavailable or malformed
+callback tables, CFI entry stubs and signed branch displacements, an invalid
+call stub, permitted BTI adjustments, rejected PAC/other adjustments, failed
+registration, unreadable and unterminated module names, pointer underflow,
+exact name matching, and stop/restart. A different live `seq_file.op` must
+still hide a selected module once the callback entry was verified; this
+preserves the Samsung BZIG behavior without accepting an unverified target.
+
+Three mutations must fail: bypass target validation, admit callbacks before
+registration validation, and restore the live table-pointer gate that caused
+the Samsung regression. The adjacent `mshow_filter` suite separately compares
+readable module-list invocations with the frozen phone-qualified handler.
+
+These are host models. They do not execute ARM64 code or establish that the
+callback table and entry can be resolved on the device. Phone validation must
+check root `/proc/modules` visibility, ordinary module rows, kallsyms behavior,
+and unload/restoration before a stable release.
